Unreleased
------------------------
Added
-----
- Programmatic popup cards: `storymap.openMarkerPopup(n)` navigates to the
  stop and opens its card (false and no navigation when disabled, out of
  range, disposed or mapless), plus `closeMarkerPopup(n?)` and
  `isPopupOpen(n)`. The marker itself exposes `openPopup()` /
  `closePopup()` / `togglePopup()` / `isPopupEnabled()` / `popupOpen`,
  and the `StoryMapMarker` type is exported for hosts
- `popupopen` / `popupclose` (`{ marker_number, current_slide }`) and a
  viewer-level `markerclick` re-fire, so hosts can tell marker clicks apart
  from programmatic navigation
- `tweetAuthor()` next to `tweetId()`: `Twitter` extracts both through the
  shared helpers instead of its own regex
- Full XYZ templates naming a known provider are credited to it
  (`tile.openstreetmap.org` and subdomains, `stadiamaps.com`,
  `openmaptiles.org`, `mapbox.com`, `arcgisonline.com`/`esri.com`,
  `carto.com`/`basemaps.cartocdn.com` — subdomains match their parent), in
  both the rendered line and the source attributions. Relative templates
  still render "Map data"

Changed
-------
- The IIIF reader/writer share `src/storymap/iiif-shared.ts`
  (`LOCATION_PROPERTIES`, JSON guards, bbox and URL checks) instead of
  three copies; the writer's Node CLI import carries an explicit `.ts`
  extension, which type-stripping resolution requires
- Map attribution comes from one provider-credit table (linked HTML and
  plain text cannot drift apart); font-theme URL helpers live in
  `src/storymap/font-css.ts` (re-exported from `StoryMap`)
- Error paths log with `console.warn`, prefixed `StoryMapJS:`, instead of
  `console.log`

0.10.8 (2026-09-29)
------------------------
Code audit: one regression, a set of latent bugs, seven stored-XSS sinks, a
disabled type check that had been hiding them, and a dependency swap.

Added
-----
- **`map_type: "none"`: a storymap with no map.** The text and the media are
  the whole story, which the map's unconditional construction used to make
  impossible. No `ol/Map` is built, so there is no tile request to consent to
  and the consent dialog omits its tile row; the overview control is hidden;
  the slider panel takes the full width, since there is no map to fade over;
  and `loaded` fires without waiting for a map that will never load. The
  accessors return their neutral values and the map setters are no-ops.
  `map_type: ""` and an absent key still mean OpenStreetMap, and `map_type:
  null` is normalised to that default instead of throwing out of the
  constructor (the schema already allowed the key to be missing)
- **`storymapToManifest()` is now a library export**, promoted out of the
  `convert:iiif` script, so one function both writes and reads the IIIF format
  and a host can produce a manifest in the browser. The CLI delegates to it
  (via Node's own type stripping, so no new dependency and no build step), and
  its output is unchanged byte for byte across all 50 fixtures
- **Annotation-driven tour stops.** A IIIF manifest whose commenting,
  tagging, classifying or describing annotations carry a fragment target
  becomes one slide per annotation, each fitting the region it points at —
  the Storiiies/Micrio shape. `text/plain` bodies become one paragraph per
  block (escaped), `text/html` keeps its markup, and a `Sound` body becomes
  the slide's media. A `PointSelector` becomes a square of 5% of the
  canvas's smaller side, centred and clamped. New `docs/iiif-authoring.md`
  (published as `authoring.html`) and a four-stop example,
  `public/examples-iiif/annotated-image.json`
- A normalized selector reader: `FragmentSelector`/`ImageApiSelector`
  (`xywh=`, including `xywh=pixel:`), `PointSelector`, `TextQuoteSelector`,
  `TimeState` and `SvgSelector` are all read, one level of `refinedBy` is
  followed, and a string target's `#xywh=` fragment counts. Quote and SVG
  selectors are preserved but not yet resolved or drawn — the guide says so
- The painting body's standard fields are the only source for the slide
  media record: `body.label` (caption), `body.requiredStatement` /
  `body.provider` (credit), `body.accessibilitySummary` (alt),
  `body.thumbnail` (thumb), and a `TextualBody` with `format: text/vtt`
  (subtitles). The `storymap:mediaCaption` / `mediaCredit` / `mediaAlt`
  extension terms are dropped; a manifest still using them loads, but the
  strings are no longer read
  (subtitles)
- `media.subtitles`: a WebVTT file rendered as a `<track kind="subtitles">`
  on the audio or video element
- Per-slide `narration: {url}`, played through one reused audio element
  rather than the slide's media frame, so switching slides cannot leave a
  recording playing. Gated by browser autoplay policy (it starts after the
  first gesture) and by a new `media:narration` consent service, so the GDPR
  mode has no hole for it
- `autoplay_media`: with autoplay on, a slide whose media is audio or video
  advances when it ends instead of on the millisecond timer. The timer stays
  armed as a fallback, so a media element that never fires `ended` cannot
  stall the story
- Per-slide `marker: { icon, iconSize, image, label, popup, audioBadge }`,
  with `location.*` kept as the legacy spelling and `marker.*` winning. No
  new `storymap:` term: in a manifest all of it arrives in the `navPlace`
  Feature `properties` bag, which is a GeoJSON foreign-member bag and needs
  no vocabulary registration
- `marker.popup`: the active marker's card toggles on click, with a sanitized
  headline and excerpt, the media thumb and a real close button. Escape and
  navigating away both close it, and the marker releases it (and its keydown
  listener) in `dispose()`. Inactive markers still navigate on click
- `marker.audioBadge`: a dot on markers whose slide has narration or audio
  media
- Browser coverage for the surfaces jsdom cannot reach, and for the changes
  that came with replacing the map engine: `imageready` against a real
  asynchronously-attached IIIF source, `dispose()` proving no tile requests
  happen after teardown, `addTo()`/`removeFrom()` re-measuring a moved map,
  the `isImageSpace()` contract, runtime option and language changes,
  per-viewer consent, `tile_source_factory`, and network-level proof that
  imagery actually reaches the map (the fixture sweep only checked that a
  container existed, so a grey map used to pass everything)
- `npm run test:e2e:all` runs the whole suite on Chromium, Firefox and WebKit
  (`E2E_ALL_BROWSERS=1`). Chromium-only stays the default, so the main CI job is
  unaffected; a separate, currently non-blocking CI job runs the matrix
- A typed OpenLayers surface: all OL types (`Map`, `View`, `Layer`,
  `Source`, ...) plus the StoryMapJS extension types are re-exported, so a
  consumer can build and type its own layers without depending on `ol`
  separately. `storymap.map` is the raw `ol/Map` and is now honestly nullable
  (`OlMap | null`) instead of an `{} as Map` placeholder
- Accessors for everything the viewer kept private:
  `getBaseLayer()`, `getOverlayLayers()`/`getOverlayLayer(i)`,
  `getOverlayCount()`, `setOverlayVisible()`/`setOverlayOpacity()`,
  `getMinimap()`, `getLine()`/`getLineActive()`, `getMarker(n)`/`getMarkers()`
  and `setExtraAttributions()`; underscore internals are no longer needed
- `tile_source_factory` may return a full `ol/layer/Layer`, not only a source,
  so a custom layer can carry its own opacity, z-index and events. A layer
  without a source (Allmaps' `WarpedMapLayer`) passes through untouched —
  the engine fields are `Layer`-typed and source reads go through a
  capability check instead of assuming a tile source
- `imageready` event (`{ source, kind, layer }`, now a typed
  `ImagereadyPayload` export with `kind: "iiif" | "zoomify" | "tiles"`)
  fired once an image source is actually ready, and re-fired on the
  storymap: `loaded` can fire before the imagery exists. The zoomify base
  layer never announced itself — only its minimap did — so a host waiting
  on the event hung on legacy stories; it fires now
- `storymap.dispose()` releases timers, the resize observer, window/document
  listeners, running slide animations, the slider and the OpenLayers map
- `isImageSpace()` tells an IIIF image presented as a picture of the world
  (`map_as_image`) from a georeferenced IIIF map; the five places that tested
  `map_type === "iiif" && map_as_image` inline now share it
- The no-op `show()`/`hide()` methods (map and marker) are marked deprecated
  with the reason, and the surviving map/marker methods are documented as
  aliases over the OpenLayers map
- `marker.createPopup()` is back as a deprecated no-op, so pre-0.10 code that
  calls it keeps working. It never did anything upstream either: the base
  `MapMarker._createPopup` had an empty body and the Leaflet override's body
  was commented out, so the `map_popup` option that called it never had an
  effect
- `map.addTo(container)` / `map.removeFrom(container)` keep their original
  `DomMixed` behaviour (append/remove the map container, fire
  `added`/`removed`) and are now OpenLayers-aware: `addTo` re-attaches the map
  target and calls `updateSize()` after the move, `removeFrom` untargets the
  map so it does not render into a detached node

Fixed
-----
- A IIIF `requiredStatement`'s `label` was discarded, so a manifest that labels
  its statement — "Credit", "Rights holder", "Photographer" — showed the bare
  value. The label is now kept ("Attribution: …") where there is one, and an
  unlabelled statement is still the bare value, unchanged
- An IIIF `Collection` was accepted as a Manifest and its member Manifests were
  read as if they were Canvases, producing text-only slides with no media, no
  locations and no warning. A Collection is now detected and reported by name
  with its members instead
- A IIIF manifest's canonical ids were dropped, so a stop could not be
  addressed by its own id even though the viewer already resolves deep links by
  `uniqueid`. Canvas `id` (falling back to the Manifest's) now lands on the
  slide, and an annotation-driven stop gets `<canvasId>#<index>` so it is
  addressable without colliding with the canvas it annotates
- A slide with body text and no headline rendered a literal "headline" as an
  `<h2>`: `Text`'s constructor seeded its data with the placeholder strings
  "headline"/"text", which `mergeData` only overwrites when the key is
  present. The empty strings its render guards already expect
- `e2e/known-issues/issue-288` tested neither half of what it claimed: without
  `hasTouch` the browser still reports `pointer: fine`, so `Browser.touch` and
  `Browser.mobile` were false and the desktop branch rendered, and the click
  landed on `.vco-slidenav-next`, which is `display: none` on mobile. It now
  enables touch, asserts the mobile branch is the one under test, checks the
  `touch-action: pan-y` rule the fix is actually about, and drives a real
  touch drag (via CDP) to show a vertical swipe scrolls the page past the
  storymap
- `e2e/known-issues/issue-452` reported a `zoom` declaration that exists in no
  stylesheet. OpenLayers ships `.ol-viewport canvas { all: unset }`, and Firefox
  and WebKit enumerate `zoom` as a *declared* property once `all` has reset it
  while Chromium does not. It now asserts against the stylesheet's source text
- The touch-scrollbar assertion in `e2e/scroll-hint` compared
  `getComputedStyle().scrollbarWidth` against `"thin"`/`"auto"`, which is not
  comparable across engines: Chromium computes `auto`, Firefox computes `none`
  for the same declaration. The declaration is now checked in the stylesheet
  source, and the runtime half is skipped where Playwright does not report
  touch (WebKit)
- A new georeferenced-IIIF fixture (`issue-iiif-geo`), because the negative
  `isImageSpace()` case could not be reached from the existing ones: the
  document sets `map_as_image`, and the document wins over a constructor option
- The consent registry keyed its stored decisions on a *localized* label, so
  the same service hashed differently in German than in English and renaming a
  label orphaned every saved decision. Keys are now stable and namespaced
  (`map:tiles`, `fonts:web`, `media:<type>`) with the localized name carried
  alongside, and existing decisions are migrated on read. The per-slide dialog
  also asked "Load content from youtube?" — it now shows the media's display
  name, matching the start-of-story dialog
- 27 of the 29 bundled locales had no `consent_*` strings at all and silently
  fell back to English, including the whole start-of-story dialog. The 9
  missing keys are translated in 23 locales; `npm run check:locales` now fails
  on a new gap (its `--strict` flag was documented but never implemented, and
  the check was not in CI) instead of only reporting
- Removing a map marker did not renumber the ones behind it, even though
  `marker_number` is the index a click hands to `goTo()`: a click on the third
  pin after removing the second navigated to the wrong slide
- Every slide got an empty element id. The generated `uniqueid` was only
  created when the field was the empty string, but it is optional in the
  schema and absent from 47 of the 48 bundled fixtures
- `dispose()` released the timers and the OpenLayers map but nothing they
  owned, so a torn-down storymap left its slider, slides, media players,
  markers and buttons in the document. Each class now releases what it owns,
  and three listeners that could not be removed at all (registered as inline
  arrows or a throwaway `.bind()`) keep a reference
- A preload was tracked in one `preloadTimer` that held either a
  `setTimeout` or a `requestIdleCallback` handle. Both are numbers, so
  cancelling one with the other's API appeared to work while leaking it
- map_bbox was ignored for every IIIF map: the runtime test was "map_type is
  iiif", so a *georeferenced* IIIF map was treated as image space and its view
  was fitted to the image extent instead of the bbox
- 12 tile/vector source sites declared `attributions: []`, so the OSM, Stadia,
  Mapbox, Esri, Carto, OpenTopo, Esri Ocean, NASA, overlay and mini-map
  credits never reached the attribution control. They are now credited from
  the source; custom `.vco-map-attribution` lines are unchanged
- Navigating mutated the caller's document: the computed marker zoom was
  written back onto `slides[i].location.zoom`, and `StoryMap.data` is that very
  object. The zoom ladder now lives in an internal store; the authored value is
  left alone and is still used for the min/max bounds
- `map_options.interactions` was documented as replacing the defaults; it is
  additive (the viewer's own pan/zoom set is always installed)
- Image media never called onLoaded(): the call had drifted into a dead
  position after a `return` in _altText(), so every image slide kept a
  permanent "Loading Image" overlay painted over it and never rendered its
  credit or caption
- Consent-denied media threw a TypeError on desktop: updateMediaDisplay()
  dereferenced `content_item.style` on the empty placeholder
- Map.goTo() fired its change event twice for a slide with no location
- start_at_slide supplied by the storymap *data* was ignored: the field was
  read before the data merge and the slider's opening goTo() fires before the
  change listener is attached
- The menubar collapse toggle was a no-op: the portrait branch it only
  appears in unconditionally overwrote the map height it asked for
- map_type "stadia" with no style resolved to a non-Stadia style name, so
  every tile 404'd; setMapOption("map_bbox") rebuilt the View without the
  deliberate constraints
- The start-of-story consent dialog listed services the visitor had already
  answered, and left answered rows greyed out rather than removing them
  (two consent e2e tests were failing because of this)
- The YouTube IFrame API poll retried forever; Vimeo's embed URL used &amp;
  separators that are not decoded in a URL property; the horizontal swipe
  constraint clamp was inverted; slide positions were computed from the
  previous width; goToId() with an unknown id jumped to slide 0
- 7 of the 18 media types had no icon glyph, so their error boxes were blank
- he.json carried a UTF-8 BOM that made it unparseable by standard JSON tools

Security
--------
- The slide-text sanitizer was a denylist that copied every attribute
  verbatim. `style` came through untouched, which allowed a pasted
  full-viewport opaque overlay over the map and controls (chrome spoofing and
  clickjacking from one attribute). It is now an allowlist; `target` is always
  forced to `_blank`, `id`/`name` are dropped, `srcset` is validated per
  candidate, and rebuilt embeds are sandboxed with a no-referrer policy
- Seven innerHTML sinks that bypassed the sanitizer are routed through it:
  media credit, media caption, the navigation headline, the call-to-action
  text, the Juxtapose iframe, and the map attribution line
- media and image link hrefs are protocol-checked, so a `javascript:` href in
  the storymap JSON is no longer a click-through XSS
- a crafted `stock:` font name resolved to an arbitrary same-origin file
- the schema validator did not implement `anyOf`, so map_bbox,
  map_overview_center and the zoomify block were accepted completely
  unvalidated; `format` and `maxLength` are now honoured

Removed
-------
- The inert option keys `less_bounce`, `map_subdomains`, `map_popup`,
  `zoom_distance`, `dragging` and `path_gfx`: documented and accepted, but read
  by nothing. They are gone from the schema, the types and the defaults, and
  documents carrying them still validate, because the schema does not close
  `additionalProperties`

Changed
-------
- The map engine is genuinely optional. `StoryMap._map` is `null` rather than a
  `{}` stub, so the accessors' `this._map ? … : neutral` guards mean what they
  say instead of passing on a truthy object and then calling a method on it,
  and the paths that only make sense with a map now throw a named error if they
  are reached without one
- `StoryMap._el.map` is `HTMLElement | null` rather than `HTMLElement`
- A second viewer on a page asking for a different `language` now **throws**
  instead of silently repainting the first one. The UI strings are a single
  page-wide binding read while a viewer is constructed, so a conflict was
  never survivable; the claim is released by `dispose()`. Only an explicit
  `language` — the constructor option or the document's — counts as a request:
  a viewer that was not given one adopts the page's current locale, so calling
  the public `setLanguage()` and then building viewers without a `language`
  option keeps the locale instead of resetting the page to English. The
  trade-off is that such a viewer follows a previous viewer's
  `refreshLanguage()`
- Several viewers on a page no longer overwrite each other's consent
  decisions: each manager merges into the shared record and re-reads it on
  every ask, instead of writing its whole set back
- With `keyboard: true` on more than one viewer, an arrow keypress goes to the
  viewer that holds focus, or else the one interacted with most recently
- External scripts and stylesheets are injected once per URL for the page, so
  two viewers no longer both load the YouTube/SoundCloud APIs and the font
  theme
- `dispose()` empties the host container and turns the public methods and
  accessors into no-ops; there is no re-init path (build a new StoryMap)
- strictNullChecks is enabled. The `{} as HTMLElement` placeholders it was
  masking are now honestly nullable, which is what the consent-denied crash
  came from
- morpheus (an inlined UMD build that shipped no types) is replaced by a
  ~130 line Web Animations API wrapper with the same call signature: one
  dependency and one hand-maintained .d.ts removed, -5 kB raw and no
  measurable change over the wire. morpheus gzipped well, so most of the raw
  saving is offset by the new code; the win here is the dependency and the
  type stub, not the bytes
- npm run check:locales reports which locales are missing which strings.
  28 of 29 are missing 8-10, mostly the per-service consent labels
- Docs corrected: DEVELOPMENT.md described a CommonJS/UMD build with a
  KLStoryMap global (it is ESM-only), the CHANGELOG claimed consent labels
  were fully translated and that zoomify was removed (both false), and
  README/index.html said slide text is rendered raw (it is sanitized)

0.10.0 - 0.10.7 (2026-09-22 to 2026-09-25)
------------------------
Released as eight patches without individual changelog entries; this is the
consolidated list. Viewer-only restructure of the repository:
- Removed the editor Flask backend, CDN staging and all legacy build artifacts
- Replaced webpack with Vite (dev/build/preview), default Vite project layout
- Converted the source to strict TypeScript (target ES2022, noImplicitAny, no explicit any)
- Replaced Leaflet with OpenLayers. IIIF Image API ("map_type": "iiif") is the
  recommended image basemap; the legacy Zoomify pyramid ("map_type": "zoomify")
  is retained as a deprecated basemap and warns when it loads
- Converted Less to SASS with zero deprecation warnings (@use only, no @import, no vendor prefixes, no postcss-scss)
- Fonts bundled via @fontsource + @fontsource-utils/scss mixins (woff2, latin subsets); no runtime font CDN requests
- New exchange format: IIIF Presentation API 3.0 manifests (docs/storymap-as-iiif-manifest.md) with navPlace geo +
  a storymap: extension; all example fixtures converted and validated against the official IIIF validator;
  StoryMap reads both legacy JSON and Presentation 3 manifests
- JSON Schema for storymap data: runtime validation with console error reporting + CLI validator
- ESLint (all rules enabled, TS mixins per the handbook replace runtime classMixin), Stylelint (zero disabled
  rules), Prettier, GitHub Actions for CI (lint/typecheck/validate/tests/build/e2e) and release packaging
- Dropped code for pre-2022 browsers: XHR -> fetch, removed vendor detection, engine workarounds, pollers,
  minEmoji sprite polyfill and Knight Lab tracking
- Bug fixes: null-offset crashes, IIIF source races, unique-id generation, swipe direction check, empty-block cleanup
- Stacked raster overlays with per-layer presentation (opacity, visibility,
  attribution, className, blendMode, lon/lat extent), runtime setters and
  setMapOption rebuild; minimap overview constraint via overview_extent
- Slide transition events (transitionstart/transitionend), guaranteed goTo
  change events, opt-in global keyboard navigation
- Runtime language switching without reload (MenuBar.refreshLabels,
  StoryMap.refreshLanguage); locale fallback no longer mutates English;
  German fullscreen labels
- Attribution refresh on basemap switch (+setExtraAttributions); identical
  credits collapse to one entry; route lines pinned above rebuilt tile
  layers; font_css resolves page-relative; slide colors accept any CSS color
- Consent: decisions persist in localStorage (storymapjs-consent, no expiry)
  instead of a 90-day cookie; a start-of-story dialog lists the external
  services the story uses (map tiles, media services, external fonts) and
  offers allow all, decline all or a decision per service, with per-service
  panels as the fallback for services discovered later. Only en.json
  translates every label: the other 28 bundled locales are each missing the
  per-service consent strings and fall back to English (npm run check:locales)
- Mobile gestures: iOS swipe navigation fixed — signed gesture deltas (a
  rightward swipe on the left half no longer counts as "next"), touchcancel
  handled without leaking touchmove/mouseleave listeners (swipes used to stop
  working after the first slide), touchend reads changedTouches, and
  touch-action: pan-y on the slider keeps vertical page scrolling
- Accessibility: image alt text end-to-end (schema, IIIF storymap:mediaAlt,
  caption-as-plain-text fallback), aria-live slide announcements, real <button>
  menubar actions with :focus-visible styling, keyboard-operable slider nav
  arrows (role/tabindex/Enter/Space), prefers-reduced-motion honored
  (instant slide changes, no autoplay), embed page allows pinch-zoom
- Image performance: offscreen slide images are lazy-loaded and
  async-decoded (upgraded to eager when their slide becomes active), IIIF
  image URLs request container-matched widths, optional srcset/sizes pass
  through for plain images
- Fonts: non-latin subsets (cyrillic, greek, hebrew, vietnamese) shipped
  from the installed Fontsource packages where a family provides them, plus a
  new stock:cairo theme with arabic coverage
- IIIF: per-slide image regions (location.region as IIIF xywh in image
  pixels) fit the view on navigation for image-map stories, encoded in
  manifests as storymap:imageRegion
- Storymap JSON: schema entries for map_area, overlays (with per-layer
  opacity, visibility, attribution, className, blendMode and lon/lat extent),
  overview_extent and keyboard — plus the slide fields location.region and
  media.alt/srcset/sizes, and a fix for overlays and overview_extent being
  silently dropped when they came from the data file instead of the options
- IIIF manifests: the same options as storymap:mapArea, storymap:overlays,
  storymap:overviewExtent and storymap:keyboard terms on the map
  configuration service, storymap:mediaSrcset / storymap:mediaSizes on the
  canvas, and the native geospatial vocabulary where IIIF has it — a navPlace
  Polygon as the story extent (map_bbox), an IIIF Image API Selector as
  location.region
- IIIF manifests: storymap:georeferencedLayers places a IIIF image on the
  geographic map from a Georeference Extension annotation body (affine
  placement of its ground control points); placements needing a
  non-affine transform or a rotated sheet are reported and skipped, so
  warped layers stay a tile_source_factory/Allmaps concern
- Map providers: tile_source_factory option injects custom OpenLayers tile
  layers/sources (e.g. WMS) for the base map, overlays and minimap, with
  recipes in the migration guide
- Embed: &autoplay=<ms> URL parameter for automatic slide playback
- Upstream audit: docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md reconciled with the implementation
  (60 of 109 triaged issues verified fixed, one-by-one verdicts and
  regression specs for each)

